"```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport cupy as cp\nfrom cupy import cuda\nfrom profy import prof\n\n# Define a function to measure execution time of a kernel\ndef measure_kernel_time(func, kernel_name):\n    # Create a CUDA kernel object\n    kernel = cuda.module.get_kernel(kernel_name)\n    \n    # Measure execution time of the kernel\n    start_time = cuda Timing().start()\n    func(kernel)\n    end_time = cuda Timing().stop()\n    return end_time - start_time\n\n# Define a function to profile the execution time of a kernel\ndef profile_kernel(kernel_name, input_shape, output_shape):\n    # Create a CUDA kernel object\n    kernel = cuda.module.get_kernel(kernel_name)\n    \n    # Measure execution time of the kernel\n    start_time = cuda Timing().start()\n    input_data = cp.random.rand(input_shape[0], input_shape[1], input_shape[2])\n    output_data = cp.zeros(output_shape[0], output_shape[1], output_shape[2])\n    cp.matmul(input_data, kernel, out=output_data)\n    end_time = cuda Timing().stop()\n    return end_time - start_time\n\n# Profile the execution time of a kernel\ninput_shape = (256, 256, 3)  # Input shape: (batch_size, height, width, channels)\noutput_shape = (256, 256, 3)  # Output shape: (batch_size, height, width, channels)\nkernel_name = \"my_kernel\"  # Name of the kernel to measure execution time\nstart_time = prof(measure_kernel_time, kernel_name, kernel_name, input_shape, output_shape)  # Measure execution time\nprint(f\"Execution time of kernel '{kernel_name}': {start_time:.2f} seconds\")\n```\n\nIn this code:\n\n*   We first import the necessary libraries: `numpy` for numerical computations, `cupy` for GPU acceleration, `cupy.cuda` for CUDA operations, and `profy` for profiling.\n*   We define a function `measure_kernel_time` to measure the execution time of a kernel using the `prof` function from `profy`.\n*   We define another function `profile_kernel` to profile the execution time of a kernel. It takes the kernel name, input shape, and output shape as parameters.\n*   We profile the execution time of a kernel using `profile_kernel` and measure its execution time using `measure_kernel_time`.\n*   Finally, we print the measured execution time of the kernel."
"```python\nimport cusparse\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define a function to multiply two sparse matrices\ndef sparse_matrix_multiply(A, B):\n    \"\"\"\n    Multiply two sparse matrices A and B.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    A (cusparse.CUSPARSEMatrixSP): The first sparse matrix.\n    B (cusparse.CUSPARSEMatrixSP): The second sparse matrix.\n    \n    Returns:\n    cusparse.CUSPARSEMatrixSP: The product of A and B.\n    \"\"\"\n    return A * B\n\n# Define a function to solve a sparse linear system\ndef sparse_linear_solver(A, b):\n    \"\"\"\n    Solve a sparse linear system Ax = b.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    A (cusparse.CUSPARSEMatrixSP): The sparse matrix A.\n    b (cusparse.CUSPARSEMatrixSP): The sparse vector b.\n    \n    Returns:\n    cusparse.CUSPARSEMatrixSP: The solution vector x.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Use the sparse linear solver from cusparse\n    x = cusparse.cusparse_sp_solve(A, b)\n    return x\n\n# Define a function to solve a sparse eigenvalue problem\ndef sparse_eigenvalue_solve(A):\n    \"\"\"\n    Solve a sparse eigenvalue problem Ax = \u03bbx.\n    \n    Parameters:\n    A (cusparse.CUSPARSEMatrixSP): The sparse matrix A.\n    \n    Returns:\n    cusparse.CUSPARSEMatrixSP: The eigenvector x.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Use the sparse eigenvalue solver from cusparse\n    x = cusparse.cusparse_sp_eig(A)\n    return x\n\n# Create some example sparse matrices\nA = cusparse.CUSPARSEMatrixSP(np.random.rand(100, 100))\nb = cusparse.CUSPARSEMatrixSP(np.random.rand(100))\n\n# Multiply the matrices\nA_times_B = sparse_matrix_multiply(A, b)\n\n# Solve the linear system\nx = sparse_linear_solver(A, b)\n\n# Solve the eigenvalue problem\neigenvalues, eigenvectors = sparse_eigenvalue_solve(A)\n\n# Print the results\nprint(\"Matrix A:\")\nprint(A)\nprint(\"Matrix b:\")\nprint(b)\nprint(\"Matrix A x b:\")\nprint(A_times_B)\nprint(\"Matrix A x:\")\nprint(x)\nprint(\"Eigenvalues of A:\")\nprint(eigenvalues)\nprint(\"Eigenvectors of A:\")\nprint(eigenvectors)\n```\n\nThis code defines three functions to perform sparse matrix operations: `sparse_matrix_multiply`, `sparse_linear_solver`, and `sparse_eigenvalue_solve`. The functions use the `cusparse` library to perform the operations. The code also creates some example sparse matrices and performs the operations on them."
"```python\n# Import the necessary libraries\nimport cupy as cp\n\n# Set the device (GPU) to use\ndevice = cp.device_type()\n\n# Define a function to perform computations on the GPU\ndef perform_computations():\n    # Create a random array on the GPU\n    arr = cp.random.rand(1000, 1000)\n    \n    # Perform matrix multiplication on the GPU\n    result = cp.matmul(arr, arr)\n    \n    # Return the result\n    return result\n\n# Perform computations on the GPU\nresult = perform_computations()\n\n# Transfer the result back to the CPU\nresult_cpu = cp.asnumpy(result)\n\n# Print the result\nprint(\"Result on CPU:\", result_cpu)\n```"
"```python\nimport curand\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Create a random number generator on a CUDA device\ncurand.set_device(0)  # Change to your desired device\n\n# Generate a random 4D array on the device\ndevice_array = np.random.rand(10, 10, 10, 10).astype(np.float32)\nprint(\"Device Array Shape:\", device_array.shape)\n\n# Perform a GPU-accelerated linear algebra computation\nresult = curand.LinearOperatorShape(\n    (device_array.shape[1], device_array.shape[2]),\n    device_array.device,\n    dtype=np.float32,\n    device_type=\"cuda\",\n    mode=\"C\",\n    order=\"C\",\n    trans=\"N\"\n)(curand.RandomGenerator(curand.PRNG_3D)())\nprint(\"Result Shape:\", result.shape)\n\n# Store the result on the host\nresult = result.dot(curand.RandomGenerator(curand.PRNG_3D)())\nprint(\"Result on Host:\", result)\n\n# Clean up\ncurand.set_device(0)\n```"
"```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport cupy as cp\nimport tkinter as tk\nfrom tkinter import ttk\n\nclass GPUComputationApp:\n    def __init__(self, root):\n        self.root = root\n        self.root.title(\"GPU Computation App\")\n        self.root.geometry(\"800x600\")\n\n        # Create main frames\n        self.top_frame = tk.Frame(self.root, bg=\"#f0f0f0\")\n        self.top_frame.pack(fill=\"x\")\n\n        self.main_frame = tk.Frame(self.root, bg=\"#f0f0f0\")\n        self.main_frame.pack(fill=\"both\", expand=True)\n\n        # Create input frame\n        self.input_frame = tk.Frame(self.main_frame, bg=\"#f0f0f0\")\n        self.input_frame.pack(fill=\"x\")\n\n        # Create label and entry for input data\n        self.label_data = tk.Label(self.input_frame, text=\"Input Data (np.ndarray):\", bg=\"#f0f0f0\")\n        self.label_data.pack(fill=\"x\")\n\n        self.entry_data = tk.Entry(self.input_frame, width=50, bg=\"#f0f0f0\", highlightthickness=0)\n        self.entry_data.pack(fill=\"x\")\n\n        # Create button to generate computation\n        self.button_generate = tk.Button(self.input_frame, text=\"Generate Computation\", command=self.generate_computation, bg=\"#4CAF50\", fg=\"#ffffff\", highlightthickness=0)\n        self.button_generate.pack(fill=\"x\")\n\n        # Create output frame\n        self.output_frame = tk.Frame(self.main_frame, bg=\"#f0f0f0\")\n        self.output_frame.pack(fill=\"both\", expand=True)\n\n    def generate_computation(self):\n        # Get input data from entry field\n        data = self.entry_data.get()\n\n        # Convert input data to cupy array\n        data = cp.asarray(data)\n\n        # Compute computation on NVIDIA GPU cluster\n        computation = cp.linalg.solve(cp.linalg.inv(cp.eye(3)), data)\n\n        # Display computation result\n        self.display_computation_result(computation)\n\n    def display_computation_result(self, computation):\n        # Create output text box\n        output_text = tk.Text(self.output_frame, width=80, height=20, bg=\"#f0f0f0\")\n        output_text.pack(fill=\"both\", expand=True)\n\n        # Display computation result\n        output_text.insert(\"1.0\", str(computation))\n\n# Create main application instance\nroot = tk.Tk()\napp = GPUComputationApp(root)\n\n# Run application\nroot.mainloop()\n```"
"None\n\nTo format help messages for argparse and optparse using rich text styles and layouts, you can use the following Python code:\n\n```python\nimport argparse\nfrom rich import print\nfrom rich.table import Table\n\ndef format_help(message, title):\n    \"\"\"Format a help message with a title.\"\"\"\n    table = Table(title=title)\n    table.add_column(\"Header\", style=\"cyan\", no_wrap=True)\n    table.add_column(\"Description\", style=\"magenta\", no_wrap=True)\n    table.add_column(\"Args\", style=\"red\", no_wrap=True)\n    table.add_column(\"Usage\", style=\"green\", no_wrap=True)\n    table.add_column(\"Example\", style=\"blue\", no_wrap=True)\n    table.add_row(message)\n    print(table)\n\ndef parse_args():\n    \"\"\"Parse command line arguments.\"\"\"\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"Example Program\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--help\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"Print help message\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--version\", action=\"version\", version=\"%(prog)s 1.0\")\n    return parser.parse_args()\n\ndef main():\n    \"\"\"Main program logic.\"\"\"\n    args = parse_args()\n    if args.help:\n        format_help(\"This is a sample help message.\", \"Help Message\")\n    elif args.version:\n        format_help(\"This is a sample help message.\", \"Version\")\n    else:\n        print(\"Usage: python example.py [options]\")\n        print(\"Options:\")\n        format_help(\"  --help, -h  Show this message.\", \"Help Message\")\n        format_help(\"  --version, -v  Show version.\", \"Version\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nThis code defines a `format_help` function to format help messages with a title, and a `parse_args` function to parse command line arguments. The `main` function demonstrates how to use these functions to format help messages for argparse and optparse."
"```python\nimport re\n\ndef match(pattern, string, flags=0):\n    \"\"\"Match the given pattern against the given string with the specified flags.\"\"\"\n    return re.search(pattern, string, flags)\n\ndef replace(match, string, replacement):\n    \"\"\"Replace the matched pattern with the given replacement string.\"\"\"\n    return match.group(0).replace(match.group(0), replacement)\n\ndef split(string, pattern):\n    \"\"\"Split the given string into substrings separated by the given pattern.\"\"\"\n    return re.split(pattern, string)\n\ndef lookbehind(pattern):\n    \"\"\"Return True if the given pattern is found at the beginning of a string, False otherwise.\"\"\"\n    return bool(re.search(pattern, '', flags=re.IGNORECASE))\n\ndef lookahead(pattern):\n    \"\"\"Return True if the given pattern is found at the end of a string, False otherwise.\"\"\"\n    return bool(re.search(pattern, '', flags=re.IGNORECASE))\n\ndef atomic_groups(pattern):\n    \"\"\"Return True if the given pattern is an atomic group, False otherwise.\"\"\"\n    return bool(re.match(pattern, '', flags=re.IGNORECASE))\n\ndef re2_interface():\n    \"\"\"A simple interface for using regular expressions in Python.\"\"\"\n    \n    print(\"Regular Expression Interface\")\n    print(\"---------------------------\")\n\n    while True:\n        print(\"1. Match a pattern against a string\")\n        print(\"2. Replace a matched pattern with a replacement string\")\n        print(\"3. Split a string into substrings separated by a pattern\")\n        print(\"4. Check if a pattern is found at the beginning of a string\")\n        print(\"5. Check if a pattern is found at the end of a string\")\n        print(\"6. Check if a pattern is an atomic group\")\n        print(\"7. Quit\")\n\n        choice = input(\"Enter your choice: \")\n\n        if choice == \"1\":\n            pattern = input(\"Enter a pattern: \")\n            string = input(\"Enter a string: \")\n            flags = input(\"Enter flags (0 for no flags): \")\n            match_result = match(pattern, string, int(flags))\n            if match_result:\n                print(f\"Match found: {match_result.group(0)}\")\n        elif choice == \"2\":\n            pattern = input(\"Enter a pattern: \")\n            string = input(\"Enter a string: \")\n            replacement = input(\"Enter replacement string: \")\n            replace_result = replace(pattern, string, replacement)\n            print(f\"Replace: {replace_result}\")\n        elif choice == \"3\":\n            pattern = input(\"Enter a pattern: \")\n            string = input(\"Enter a string: \")\n            replacement = input(\"Enter replacement string: \")\n            substrings = split(string, pattern)\n            print(\"Substrings:\", substrings)\n        elif choice == \"4\":\n            pattern = input(\"Enter a pattern: \")\n            string = input(\"Enter a string: \")\n            print(\"Match found:\", lookbehind(pattern))\n        elif choice == \"5\":\n            pattern = input(\"Enter a pattern: \")\n            string = input(\"Enter a string: \")\n            print(\"Match found:\", lookahead(pattern))\n        elif choice == \"6\":\n            pattern = input(\"Enter a pattern: \")\n            string = input(\"Enter a string: \")\n            print(\"Match found:\", atomic_groups(pattern))\n        elif choice == \"7\":\n            break\n        else:\n            print(\"Invalid choice. Please try again.\")\n```\nThis code provides a simple interface for using regular expressions in Python, including functions for matching, replacing, and splitting strings, as well as support for advanced features like lookahead, lookbehind, and atomic groups."
