"You can use the `pygments` library to parse the script and the `ast` module to generate the equivalent Python code. Here is an example of how you can use these libraries:\n\n```python\nimport ast\nimport pygments\nimport pygments.lexers\nimport pygments.formatters\n\n# Define the input script\nscript = '''\n# This is a Python script written in a programming language other than Python.\n# The script will be parsed and converted into an equivalent Python script.\n# For example, a C script can be converted into a Python script using this library.\n# C code:\n#   x = 5\n#   y = 10\n#   result = x + y\n# Python code:\n#   x = 5\n#   y = 10\n#   result = x + y\n'''\n\n# Parse the script using the `ast` module\ntree = ast.parse(script)\n\n# Define a lexer for the script\nlexer = pygments.lexers.PygmentsLexer()\nlexer.add_file(lexer)\n\n# Define a formatter for the script\nformatter = pygments.formatters.HtmlFormatter()\n\n# Generate the equivalent Python code\noutput = ''\nfor node in ast.walk(tree):\n    # If the node is a function definition, convert it to a Python function\n    if isinstance(node, ast.FunctionDef):\n        func_name = node.name\n        func_code = ''\n        for stmt in node.body:\n            if isinstance(stmt, ast.Assign):\n                var_name = stmt.targets[0].id\n                value = stmt.value\n                if isinstance(value, ast.Num):\n                    value = str(value.n)\n                func_code += f'    def {func_name}({var_name}):\\n        return {value}\\n\\n'\n        output += func_code\n    # If the node is a print statement, convert it to a Python print statement\n    elif isinstance(node, ast.Print):\n        print_stmt = node.value\n        if isinstance(print_stmt, ast.Str):\n            print_stmt = print_stmt.s\n        output += f'    print({print_stmt})\\n\\n'\n    # Otherwise, add the node's source code to the output\n    else:\n        output += ast.unparse(node) + '\\n\\n'\n\nprint(output)\n```\n\nThis code takes a script written in a programming language other than Python, parses it, and converts it into an equivalent Python script. The script uses the `ast` module to parse the input script, and the `pygments` library to generate the equivalent Python code. The resulting Python code is then printed to the console."
