"```python\nimport slugify\n\ndef slugify_file(filename):\n    \"\"\"\n    Creates a stub file with a slugified version of a given string.\n\n    Args:\n        filename (str): The name of the file to be created.\n\n    Returns:\n        None\n    \"\"\"\n    # Open the file in write mode\n    with open(filename, 'w') as file:\n        # Read the content of the file\n        content = file.read()\n        \n        # Use slugify to slugify the content\n        slugified_content = slugify.slugify(content)\n        \n        # Write the slugified content to the file\n        file.write(slugified_content)\n\n# Example usage:\nslugify_file('stub.txt')\n```"
"**Session Management with JWT and Cookies using FastAPI and Starlette**\n===========================================================\n\nHere's an example of advanced session management functionality for Starlette and FastAPI using JSON Web Tokens (JWT) and secure cookie-based authentication:\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Response, HTTPException, Depends, Security\nfrom starlette.security import OAuth2PasswordBearer, OAuth2PasswordRequestForm\nfrom starlette.responses import JSONResponse\nfrom starlette.requests import Request\nfrom starlette.responses import RedirectResponse\nfrom starlette.http import HTTPStatus\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom pydantic import EmailStr\nfrom jose import jwt, jwt_decode, JWTError\nfrom fastapi.security import OAuth2PasswordBearer, OAuth2PasswordRequestForm\nfrom pydantic import EmailStr\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n# In-memory session store for demonstration purposes\nin_memory_session = {}\n\n# Security scheme for JWT authentication\noauth2_scheme = OAuth2PasswordBearer(tokenUrl=\"token\")\n\nclass TokenAuth(BaseModel):\n    access_token: str\n    token_type: str\n\nclass TokenRequestForm(BaseModel):\n    email: EmailStr\n    password: str\n\n# Function to authenticate user using JWT\ndef authenticate_user(request: Request, form: TokenRequestForm):\n    # In-memory session store for demonstration purposes\n    in_memory_session[form.email] = jwt_decode(form.password, secret_key=\"your_secret_key\", algorithms=[\"HS256\"])\n\n    # Check if user is authenticated\n    if in_memory_session[form.email] is None:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=HTTPStatus.UNAUTHORIZED, detail=\"Invalid email or password\")\n\n    # Return JWT token\n    return TokenAuth(access_token=in_memory_session[form.email][\"access_token\"], token_type=\"bearer\")\n\n# Route for token endpoint\n@app.post(\"/token\", response_model=TokenAuth)\nasync def login(form: TokenRequestForm):\n    try:\n        # Authenticate user\n        user = authenticate_user(request, form)\n        # Return JWT token\n        return user\n    except JWTError as e:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=HTTPStatus.UNAUTHORIZED, detail=\"Invalid email or password\")\n\n# In-memory session store for demonstration purposes\nin_memory_session = {}\n\n# Route for protected endpoint\n@app.get(\"/protected\", security=oauth2_scheme, dependencies=[Security(lambda x: x.context.get_token_info().get(\"sub\") == \"your_sub_token\")])\nasync def protected_route(user: str = Depends(Security(lambda x: x.context.get_token_info().get(\"sub\") == \"your_sub_token\"))):\n    # Check if user is authenticated\n    if user is None:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=HTTPStatus.UNAUTHORIZED, detail=\"Unauthorized\")\n    # Return user details\n    return {\"user\": user}\n```\n\nIn this code:\n\n*   We define a `TokenAuth` model to represent the JWT token.\n*   We define a `TokenRequestForm` model to validate the JWT token.\n*   We implement the `authenticate_user` function to authenticate users using JWT.\n*   We define a `/token` endpoint for token validation.\n*   We define a `/protected` endpoint with authentication using the `oauth2_scheme` security scheme and the `Security` decorator. The `dependencies` parameter is used to specify the authentication scheme for the endpoint.\n\nRemember to replace `\"your_secret_key\"` with your actual secret key for JWT authentication."
"```python\nimport praat\n\nclass SpeechAnalyzer:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.praat_object = praat.Praat('C:\\\\Program Files\\\\Praat\\\\praat-4.0.0.exe', 'C:\\\\Program Files\\\\Praat\\\\praat-data')\n\n    def open_audio_file(self, audio_file):\n        \"\"\"Open an audio file and return its parameters.\"\"\"\n        return self.praat_object.open(audio_file)\n\n    def analyze_speech(self, audio_file):\n        \"\"\"Analyze the speech patterns in an audio file.\"\"\"\n        return self.praat_object.analyze_speech(audio_file)\n\n    def generate_visualization(self, audio_file):\n        \"\"\"Generate a visualization of the speech data in an audio file.\"\"\"\n        return self.praat_object.generate_visualization(audio_file)\n\n# Example usage:\nanalyzer = SpeechAnalyzer()\naudio_file = 'path_to_your_audio_file.wav'\nanalysis = analyzer.analyze_speech(audio_file)\nvisualization = analyzer.generate_visualization(audio_file)\n```\n\nNote: This code requires Praat to be installed and its executable to be in the system's PATH. Additionally, you'll need to provide the path to your audio file when calling the `analyze_speech` and `generate_visualization` methods."
"```python\n# Import necessary libraries\nfrom prefect import tasks, flow\nfrom prefect.tasks import aws\nfrom prefect.tasks.s3 import bucket_management\nfrom prefect.tasks.ec2 import instance_creation\n\n# Define the Prefect task queue\ndef run_tasks():\n    # Create an EC2 instance\n    instance = instance_creation.create_ec2_instance(\n        name=\"MyEC2Instance\",\n        image=\"ami-0c94855ba95c71c99\",\n        instance_type=\"t2.micro\",\n        key_pair=\"my_key_pair\",\n    )\n\n    # Create an S3 bucket\n    bucket = bucket_management.create_s3_bucket(\n        name=\"MyS3Bucket\",\n        bucket_name=\"my-bucket\",\n        acl=\"private\",\n    )\n\n    # Upload a file to the S3 bucket\n    bucket_management.upload_file(\n        source=\"path/to/file.txt\",\n        bucket=bucket.name,\n        filename=\"file.txt\",\n        acl=\"private\",\n    )\n\n# Set up the Prefect task queue\n@tasks.task\ndef run_tasks_queue():\n    run_tasks()\n\n# Set up the AWS integration\naws.init()\n```"
