"None\n\nTo set up authentication for using the Google Drive API, you'll need to follow these steps:\n\nFirst, you'll need to create a project in the Google Cloud Console. You can do this by going to the [Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/) and following these steps:\n\n1. Create a new project by clicking on the \"Select a project\" field and entering a project name.\n2. Click on the \"Create\" button to create the project.\n3. Click on the \"Navigation menu\" ( three horizontal lines in the top left corner) and select \"APIs & Services\" > \"Dashboard\".\n4. Click on the \"Enable APIs and Services\" button.\n5. Search for \"Drive\" and click on the result.\n6. Click on the \"Enable\" button.\n\nNext, you'll need to create credentials for your project. You can do this by clicking on the \"Navigation menu\" (three horizontal lines in the top left corner) and selecting \"APIs & Services\" > \"Credentials\". Click on the \"Create Credentials\" button and select \"OAuth client ID\".\n\nFor the OAuth client ID, select \"Other\" and enter a name for your client ID. You can also choose to use the \"Web application\" or \"Service account\" options.\n\nOnce you've created the credentials, you'll need to install the Google API Client Library for Python in your project. You can do this by running the following command in your terminal:\n\n```\npip install google-api-python-client\n```\n\nNow, you can set up authentication for your application by creating a function that imports the necessary libraries and uses the OAuth2 client library to authenticate with the Google Drive API. Here's an example:\n\n```python\nfrom google.oauth2 import service_account\nfrom googleapiclient.discovery import build\n\ndef get_service_account_credentials():\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a service account credentials object.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Create credentials from the JSON key file\n    credentials = service_account.Credentials.from_service_account_file(\n        'path_to_your_service_account_key.json'\n    )\n\n    # Create a client instance with the credentials\n    service = build('drive', 'v3', credentials=credentials)\n\n    return service\n\ndef authenticate_with_drive_service():\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a service account credentials object.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Get a service account credentials object\n    service_account_credentials = get_service_account_credentials()\n\n    # Authenticate with the Google Drive API\n    drive_service = service_account_credentials.service\n\n    # You can now use the drive_service object to access the Google Drive API\n    return drive_service\n\n# Example usage:\ndrive_service = authenticate_with_drive_service()\n```\n\nNote: You'll need to replace `'path_to_your_service_account_key.json'` with the actual path to your service account key file."
"```python\nfrom gitpython import Git\n\nclass GitIgnoreParser:\n    def __init__(self, repo):\n        self.repo = Git(repo)\n\n    def get_ignored_patterns(self):\n        ignored_patterns = self.repo.cwd().gitignore\n        return ignored_patterns.splitlines()\n\ndef main():\n    repo_path = 'path/to/your/repo'  # replace with your repo path\n    parser = GitIgnoreParser(repo_path)\n    ignored_patterns = parser.get_ignored_patterns()\n    print(ignored_patterns)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n```"
"```python\nimport os\nimport json\nfrom langsmith_client import LangsmithClient\n\n# Replace with your LangSmith API credentials\nlangsmith_api_key = \"YOUR_API_KEY\"\nlangsmith_api_secret = \"YOUR_API_SECRET\"\n\n# Replace with your LangSmith endpoint URL\nlangsmith_endpoint_url = \"https://your-endpoint-url.langsmith.io\"\n\n# Create a new Langsmith client instance\nclient = LangsmithClient(\n    api_key=langsmith_api_key,\n    api_secret=langsmith_api_secret,\n    endpoint_url=langsmith_endpoint_url\n)\n\n# Establish a connection to the LangSmith LLM Tracing and Evaluation Platform\nconnection = client.connect()\n```\n\nReplace `\"YOUR_API_KEY\"`, `\"YOUR_API_SECRET\"` and `\"your-endpoint-url.langsmith.io\"` with your actual LangSmith API credentials and endpoint URL respectively."
"```python\n# Import necessary libraries\nimport gym\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Create a CartPole environment\nenv = gym.make('CartPole-v1')\n\n# Define the agent class\nclass CartPoleAgent:\n    def __init__(self, env, learning_rate=0.001, gamma=0.99, epsilon=0.1):\n        self.env = env\n        self.q_table = {}\n        self.learning_rate = learning_rate\n        self.gamma = gamma\n        self.epsilon = epsilon\n\n    # Method to select action\n    def select_action(self, state):\n        if np.random.rand() < self.epsilon:\n            return env.action_space.sample()\n        else:\n            q_values = self.get_q_values(state)\n            return np.argmax(q_values)\n\n    # Method to get q values\n    def get_q_values(self, state):\n        q_values = {}\n        for a in range(self.env.action_space.n):\n            action = a\n            next_state, reward, done, _ = self.env.step(action)\n            q_values[a] = self.env.get_reward_function()(next_state) + self.gamma * np.max(self.env.get_q_values(next_state))\n        return q_values\n\n    # Method to train the agent\n    def train(self, num_episodes):\n        for episode in range(num_episodes):\n            state = env.reset()\n            done = False\n            rewards = 0\n            while not done:\n                action = self.select_action(state)\n                next_state, reward, done, _ = self.env.step(action)\n                rewards += reward\n                self.q_table[state] += self.learning_rate * (reward + self.gamma * np.max(self.env.get_q_values(next_state)) - self.q_table[state])\n                state = next_state\n\n    # Method to render the environment\n    def render(self, state):\n        return env.render('CartPole-v1')\n\n# Create and train the agent\nagent = CartPoleAgent(env)\nagent.train(1000)\n\n# Render the environment\nagent.render(env.reset())\n```"
"```python\n# Import the necessary libraries\nimport azure.graphclient as graph_client\nfrom typing import Optional\n\n# Set up the Azure subscription and resource group\nsubscription_id = \"your_subscription_id\"\nresource_group_name = \"your_resource_group_name\"\n\n# Create a graph client object\ngraph_client.set_query_cache_enabled(False)\n\ndef create_resource(resource_name: str, resource_type: str, resource_data: dict):\n    \"\"\"\n    Create a new Azure resource using the Azure Resource Graph Client Library.\n    \n    Args:\n        resource_name (str): The name of the resource to create.\n        resource_type (str): The type of the resource to create.\n        resource_data (dict): The data to fill in the resource.\n    \n    Returns:\n        dict: The created resource.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Create a new graph client object\n    graph_client = graph_client.GraphClient(subscription_id)\n    \n    # Create a new resource operation\n    resource_operation = graph_client.begin_create_or_update(\n        resource_type,\n        resource_name,\n        resource_data\n    )\n    \n    # Wait for the operation to complete\n    resource_operation.execute()\n    \n    # Return the created resource\n    return resource_operation.result\n\ndef update_resource(resource_name: str, resource_type: str, resource_data: dict):\n    \"\"\"\n    Update an existing Azure resource using the Azure Resource Graph Client Library.\n    \n    Args:\n        resource_name (str): The name of the resource to update.\n        resource_type (str): The type of the resource to update.\n        resource_data (dict): The data to fill in the resource.\n    \n    Returns:\n        dict: The updated resource.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Create a new graph client object\n    graph_client = graph_client.GraphClient(subscription_id)\n    \n    # Find the resource operation\n    resource_operation = graph_client.begin_get_or_create_or_update(\n        resource_type,\n        resource_name,\n        resource_data\n    )\n    \n    # Update the resource operation\n    resource_operation = graph_client.begin_update(\n        resource_type,\n        resource_name,\n        resource_data\n    )\n    \n    # Wait for the operation to complete\n    resource_operation.execute()\n    \n    # Return the updated resource\n    return resource_operation.result\n\ndef delete_resource(resource_name: str, resource_type: str):\n    \"\"\"\n    Delete an existing Azure resource using the Azure Resource Graph Client Library.\n    \n    Args:\n        resource_name (str): The name of the resource to delete.\n        resource_type (str): The type of the resource to delete.\n    \n    Returns:\n        dict: The deleted resource.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Create a new graph client object\n    graph_client = graph_client.GraphClient(subscription_id)\n    \n    # Find the resource operation\n    resource_operation = graph_client.begin_delete_or_update(\n        resource_type,\n        resource_name\n    )\n    \n    # Wait for the operation to complete\n    resource_operation.execute()\n    \n    # Return the deleted resource\n    return resource_operation.result\n\ndef get_resource(resource_name: str, resource_type: str):\n    \"\"\"\n    Retrieve an existing Azure resource using the Azure Resource Graph Client Library.\n    \n    Args:\n        resource_name (str): The name of the resource to retrieve.\n        resource_type (str): The type of the resource to retrieve.\n    \n    Returns:\n        dict: The retrieved resource.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Create a new graph client object\n    graph_client = graph_client.GraphClient(subscription_id)\n    \n    # Find the resource operation\n    resource_operation = graph_client.begin_get_or_create_or_update(\n        resource_type,\n        resource_name\n    )\n    \n    # Wait for the operation to complete\n    resource_operation.execute()\n    \n    # Return the retrieved resource\n    return resource_operation.result\n```\n\nYou can use the above functions to perform CRUD operations on Azure resources. For example:\n\n```python\n# Create a new resource\nresource_name = \"my_resource\"\nresource_type = \"microsoft.compute/v2018/manageddisks\"\nresource_data = {\"name\": \"my_disk\", \"sizeInGb\": 10}\n\nresult = create_resource(resource_name, resource_type, resource_data)\nprint(result)\n\n# Update an existing resource\nresource_name = \"my_resource\"\nresource_type = \"microsoft.compute/v2018/manageddisks\"\nresource_data = {\"name\": \"my_disk\", \"sizeInGb\": 20}\n\nresult = update_resource(resource_name, resource_type, resource_data)\nprint(result)\n\n# Delete an existing resource\nresource_name = \"my_resource\"\nresource_type = \"microsoft.compute/v2018/manageddisks\"\n\nresult = delete_resource(resource_name, resource_type)\nprint(result)\n\n# Retrieve an existing resource\nresource_name = \"my_resource\"\nresource_type = \"microsoft.compute/v2018/manageddisks\"\n\nresult = get_resource(resource_name, resource_type)\nprint(result)\n```"
