"```python\n# Import the necessary packages\nimport setuptools\n\n# Set up the package\nsetuptools.setup(\n    name='my-python-project',\n    version='1.0',\n    packages=setuptools.find_packages(),\n    install_requires=[],\n    author='Your Name',\n    author_email='your@email.com',\n    description='A minimal Python project',\n    long_description='This is a minimal description of the project.',\n    url='https://github.com/your_username/my-python-project',\n    classifiers=[\n        'Programming Language :: Python :: 3',\n        'Intended Audience :: Developers',\n        'License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License',\n        'Operating System :: OS Independent'\n    ],\n    python_requires='>=3.6',\n    include_package_data=True,\n    packages=setuptools.find_packages(),\n    entry_points={\n        'console_scripts': [\n            'my_project = my_project:main'\n        ]\n    }\n)\n```\n\nYou can install the package using pip:\n\n```bash\npip install .  # Replace . with the path to the package\n```\n\nTo build the wheel file, run the following command:\n\n```bash\npython setup.py bdist_wheel\n```\n\nTo configure the `ci.py` script to run the build process on a continuous integration system, you can create a new file `ci.py` in the root directory of your project:\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport subprocess\n\ndef build_project():\n    # Run the build process here\n    subprocess.run(['pip', 'install', 'setuptools', 'wheel'], check=True)\n    subprocess.run(['python', 'setup.py', 'bdist_wheel'], check=True)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    build_project()\n```\n\nTo configure the `ci.py` script to run the build process on a continuous integration system, you can add the following line to the `setup.py` file:\n\n```python\n[metadata]\nbuild_system='wheel'\nbuild_files=['dist/*']\n```\n\nThis will configure the `ci.py` script to run the build process on a continuous integration system using the `setuptools` and `wheel` packages."
