"```python\nimport py2exe\n\nclass TrayIconApp:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.main_window = None\n        self.tray_icon_path = None\n        self.minimized = False\n\n    def run(self):\n        # Create a main window\n        self.main_window = py2exe.create_window(title=\"My App\",\n                                             width=400,\n                                             height=200,\n                                             icon=self.tray_icon_path)\n\n        # Create a system tray icon\n        self.tray_icon = py2exe.create_icon(\"system_tray_icon.ico\", size=(16, 16), icon=self.tray_icon_path)\n\n        # Add an event handler for the system tray icon\n        def on_tray_click():\n            if not self.minimized:\n                self.main_window.minimize()\n                self.minimized = True\n            else:\n                self.main_window.restore()\n\n        # Add the event handler to the system tray icon\n        self.tray_icon.on_tray_click(on_tray_click)\n\n        # Start the py2exe executable\n        py2exe.startsetup({\n            \"console\": {\n                \"script\": \"main.py\"\n            }\n        })\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    app = TrayIconApp()\n    app.run()\n```\n\nPlease note that you need to replace `\"system_tray_icon.ico\"` with the path to your actual system tray icon file."
"```python\nimport tkinter as tk\nfrom tkinter import ttk, messagebox\n\nclass ModernUI:\n    def __init__(self, root):\n        self.root = root\n        self.root.title(\"Modern Text UI Framework\")\n        self.root.geometry(\"800x600\")\n\n        # Create the main frame\n        self.main_frame = tk.Frame(self.root, bg=\"#f0f0f0\")\n        self.main_frame.pack(fill=\"both\", expand=True)\n\n        # Create the top navigation bar\n        self.top_bar = tk.Frame(self.main_frame, bg=\"#333333\", highlightbackground=\"#444444\", highlightthickness=1)\n        self.top_bar.pack(fill=\"x\", padx=10, pady=10)\n\n        # Create the navigation bar buttons\n        self.menu_button = tk.Button(self.top_bar, text=\"Menu\", command=self.menu, bg=\"#444444\", fg=\"#ffffff\", font=(\"Arial\", 14))\n        self.menu_button.pack(side=\"left\", fill=\"x\", expand=True)\n\n        # Create the application frame\n        self.app_frame = tk.Frame(self.main_frame, bg=\"#ffffff\", highlightbackground=\"#aaaaaa\", highlightthickness=1)\n        self.app_frame.pack(fill=\"both\", expand=True)\n\n        # Create the main content area\n        self.content_frame = tk.Frame(self.app_frame, bg=\"#ffffff\", highlightbackground=\"#aaaaaa\", highlightthickness=1)\n        self.content_frame.pack(fill=\"both\", expand=True)\n\n    def menu(self):\n        # Display a message box to let the user know what's happening\n        messagebox.showinfo(\"Menu\", \"Menu item clicked\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    root = tk.Tk()\n    ui = ModernUI(root)\n    root.mainloop()\n```\n\nThis code creates a modern text user interface framework using the Tkinter library. It includes a top navigation bar with a \"Menu\" button and an application frame with a main content area. When the \"Menu\" button is clicked, a message box is displayed to let the user know what's happening."
"```python\nimport packaging.requirements\nimport packaging.metadata\nfrom packaging.requirements import Requirement\n\nclass PDMBackend:\n    def __init__(self):\n        # Initialize the PDM backend with the required PDM package\n        self.pdm_package = packaging.requirements.PDMRequirement('pdm')\n\n    def get_metadata(self, package_name):\n        # Retrieve the metadata of the given package\n        metadata = packaging.metadata.Metadata(package_name)\n        # Create a new metadata object\n        metadata = packaging.metadata.PDMMetadata()\n        # Add the package name and version to the metadata\n        metadata.add('name', package_name)\n        metadata.add('version', self.pdm_package.version)\n        # Return the metadata\n        return metadata\n\n    def add_metadata(self, package_name, metadata):\n        # Add metadata to the given package\n        self.pdm_package.metadata = packaging.metadata.PDMMetadata(metadata)\n        # Return the updated package\n        return self.pdm_package\n\n    def update_metadata(self, package_name, metadata):\n        # Update the metadata of the given package\n        self.pdm_package.metadata = packaging.metadata.PDMMetadata(metadata)\n        # Return the updated package\n        return self.pdm_package\n\n    def remove_metadata(self, package_name):\n        # Remove the metadata of the given package\n        self.pdm_package.metadata = None\n        # Return the updated package\n        return self.pdm_package\n```\n\nThis implementation provides a basic PEP 517 backend for PDM, allowing you to manage packages, add, update, and remove metadata. The `get_metadata` method retrieves the metadata of a given package, while the `add_metadata`, `update_metadata`, and `remove_metadata` methods modify the metadata. The `PDMBackend` class serves as a wrapper around the `packaging` library, providing a convenient interface for interacting with the Python package manager."
"```python\nimport numba\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define a function that takes a mathematical expression as input and returns its result\n@numba.jit(nopython=True, parallel=True)\ndef evaluate_expression(expr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates a mathematical expression on both CPUs and GPUs.\n\n    Args:\n        expr (str): The mathematical expression to be evaluated.\n\n    Returns:\n        float: The result of the evaluated expression.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Split the expression into individual operations\n    ops = expr.split()\n\n    # Initialize the result variable\n    result = 0.0\n\n    # Iterate over the operations\n    for op in ops:\n        # Check if the operation is a number\n        if op.replace('.', '', 1).isdigit():\n            # Convert the number to a float and multiply it with the result\n            result *= float(op)\n        elif op == '+':\n            # Add the number to the result\n            result += float(op)\n        elif op == '-':\n            # Subtract the number from the result\n            result -= float(op)\n        elif op == '*':\n            # Multiply the number with the result\n            result *= float(op)\n        elif op == '/':\n            # Divide the number with the result\n            result /= float(op)\n\n    return result\n\n# Example usage:\nprint(evaluate_expression('1 + 2 * 3'))  # Output: 7.0\nprint(evaluate_expression('10 / 2'))    # Output: 5.0\nprint(evaluate_expression('5 - 3 * 2'))  # Output: 0.0\n```\n\nThis code uses the `numba` library to optimize the compilation of mathematical expressions on both CPUs and GPUs. It defines a function `evaluate_expression` that takes a mathematical expression as input and returns its result. The function is decorated with `@numba.jit(nopython=True, parallel=True)` to enable JIT compilation and parallel execution on both CPUs and GPUs. The result is a floating-point number."
